## Increment 1 of the concurrency adoption (§4.1 foundation):
## a future that is DRAINED (never completed) must run its `def drop`.
## Pre-fix, `ruxen_executor_drain_remaining` freed the queue entry but
## NEVER called the future's user `def drop` (Drop was not runtime-
## dispatched), so the sentinel below would NOT print. The fix routes
## the drop through a Drop vtable slot added to the future's EXISTING
## class_info (the NARROW path — no global `mixin Drop dispatch runtime`,
## no layout change to non-future classes). The drain now calls
## `Drop_dynamic_drop(future_ptr)` per still-queued future.
## See docs/dev/concurrency-impl-plan.md (Increment 1) and the drain
## hook in library/std/future/runtime/scheduler.c.
##
## Shape: the driver spawns a `LeakSentinel` future that stays Pending
## forever (it never completes), then returns Ready(0) immediately.
## After block_on, the sentinel is still on the scheduler queue. The
## program then calls `ruxen_executor_drain_remaining()` explicitly
## (the v1 teardown surface — the automatic block_on-exit/Scope drain
## is a follow-up tied to §4.2 task ownership). Drain fires
## `LeakSentinel_drop` via `Drop_dynamic_drop`; the printed line is the
## OBSERVABLE proof the drop ran on a drained, never-completed future.

class LeakSentinel
  payload: Int
  include Future
  type Output = Int

  def init(p: Int)
    self.payload = p
  end

  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Int]
    # Never completes: always Pending. It exists only to be drained.
    Poll.Pending
  end

  def drop
    puts "dropped on drain payload=#{self.payload}"
  end
end

## Driver: spawn one never-completing sentinel, then return Ready(0)
## right away. block_on exits with the sentinel still on the queue.
async def driver -> Int
  let f = LeakSentinel.new(7)
  let h = Task.spawn_raw(f)
  0
end

def main
  let r = block_on(driver())
  puts "driver returned #{r}"
  # Explicit teardown: drain the still-queued sentinel. Its `def drop`
  # fires via Drop_dynamic_drop — the Increment 1 mechanism.
  ruxen_executor_drain_remaining()
  puts "drained"
end
